21309	{"artifactId":"LLD-bfe98ff7f97178cf-s2","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-bfe98ff7f97178cf-s2-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:controller","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-01T13:49:23.980Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"pass","summary":"review: pass","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"pass","findings":[{"claimId":"r1","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"FunctionalRequirement is declared in src/workflow/artifacts/functional-definition.ts with exactly five members — id, statement, rationale?, scope ('doc'|'item') and itemRef? — and FunctionalDefinition is exactly { requirements: readonly FunctionalRequirement[] }, so the LLD's claim that id, statement and rationale are the only displayable fields and that scope/itemRef carry the grouping is complete rather than partial.","evidence":"`export interface FunctionalRequirement` and `export interface FunctionalDefinition` each resolve to exactly ONE declaration in src/workflow/artifacts/functional-definition.ts (:26 and :38), and all three read anchors confirm verbatim, including :32 `readonly scope: 'doc' | 'item';`. The member set the LLD treats as complete is the member set the source declares.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r2","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"validateFunctionalDefinition is an ASSEMBLY-time check that is not invoked anywhere on the artifact read path — in particular src/workflow/artifact-content.ts does not call it — which is the premise behind the LLD treating the projected record as structurally untrusted on arrival.","evidence":"The only non-doc, non-test call sites in the `validateFunctionalDefinition` match set are src/workflow/orchestrator.ts:145 (import), :1048 (comment) and :1097 (the call, with the known story/task ids). src/workflow/artifact-content.ts appears NOWHERE in the match set, so the read path genuinely performs no validation. Reads confirm the declaration at :54 and the absent-safe guard at :58 (`if (fd === undefined || fd.requirements.length === 0) return null;`), which is the convention the LLD adopts verbatim for ac4.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"renderFunctionalRequirementsSection in src/workflow/artifacts/functional-definition.ts generates the Functional-requirements prose from the record: a '## Functional requirements' heading plus one bullet per requirement in the form '- **<id>** — <statement>' with the rationale in italics, doc-level requirements first and per-item requirements grouped under a '**<itemRef>:**' line. This is the rendering the LLD says the webview replaces on screen.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim at every anchor: :87 the exported function; :90 the bullet template `- **${r.id}** — ${r.statement}${... _(${r.rationale})_ ...}` (a single repo-wide match); :92 `const lines: string[] = ['## Functional requirements', '']`; :105 `lines.push('', `**${item}:**`, '')` for the per-item group header. The prose rendering the LLD replaces on screen emits exactly the three fields the new renderer must carry across, so the substitution is lossless as claimed.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r4","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A per-item requirement whose itemRef is missing or empty is grouped under the literal key '(unassigned)' by the existing prose renderer, which is the behaviour the LLD's edge case says the new renderer must agree with rather than re-invent.","evidence":"Single repo-wide code match, read confirmed at src/workflow/artifacts/functional-definition.ts:99: `const key = r.itemRef !== undefined && r.itemRef.length > 0 ? r.itemRef : '(unassigned)';`. The existing renderer does bucket an orphan per-item requirement under that literal key, so the LLD's edge case prescribing agreement with it — rather than a second convention — is grounded.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r5","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The document format spine declares the functional-requirements section with the heading text 'Functional requirements' and source 'fr', and frBodyLines strips the renderer's own hard-coded heading with raw.slice(2) precisely so the engine can supply a NUMBERED heading — which is why the LLD matches the anchor title by its tail rather than by equality.","evidence":"`heading: 'Functional requirements'` with `source: 'fr'` resolves in all four formats (formats.ts:36, :58, :79, :100), confirming the LLD's statement that the spine declares the section for define/hld/lld/plan. frBodyLines is confirmed at bindings.ts:23 and the heading strip at :27 `const body = raw.slice(2);` (single repo-wide match). The numbered-heading consequence — and therefore the LLD's decision to tail-match the anchor title rather than compare it for equality — follows directly from that strip.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r6","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"ArtifactReviewView.renderedMarkdown is the artifact's own .md file read VERBATIM via readFileSync with no transformation, and the four structured body records are projected onto the same response by structuredRecords(body) with no validation — the two premises the LLD's 'the pane shows the document as written' invariant and its untrusted-record handling both rest on.","evidence":"All three probes single-match and all three reads confirmed: artifact-content.ts:52 `readonly renderedMarkdown: string;   // the artifact's own .md read verbatim (k5/lc1)`, :140 `renderedMarkdown = readFileSync(realMd, 'utf8');`, :184 `...structuredRecords(body),`. Both load-bearing premises hold at source: the body shown is the file as written, and the structured records are projected alongside it without validation.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r7","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"DocsContent in the VS Code plugin declares functionalDefinition by INDEXING off the daemon type as ArtifactReviewView['functionalDefinition'] rather than restating the record shape, and content() forwards it with a conditional spread so an absent record stays an absent KEY — the existing convention the LLD extends one hop further to the webview message.","evidence":"Both probes are single code matches and both reads now land exactly: docs-review-client.ts:34 `readonly functionalDefinition?: ArtifactReviewView['functionalDefinition'];` (the indexed declaration that prevents drift) and :109 `...(res.functionalDefinition !== undefined ? { functionalDefinition: res.functionalDefinition } : {})` (the absent-key spread). The convention the LLD extends one hop further to the webview message is the convention already in force at the IPC boundary.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r8","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The record currently stops at the extension host: vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-panel.ts contains no occurrence of 'functionalDefinition', and the docs-content variant in vscode-plugin/src/chat/protocol.ts has no field for it — so the host→webview hop the LLD adds is genuinely missing rather than already present. (An unqualified identifier grep over this repo is swamped by the docs/ artifact tree, so this absence must be re-derived path-scoped.)","evidence":"Re-probed path-scoped this time rather than with a bare identifier grep. The docs-content variant's tail is confirmed by the reads at protocol.ts:70 (`readonly type: 'docs-content';`), :80 (`readonly sections?: SectionIndex | undefined;`) and :85 (`readonly degradation?: RenderDegradation | undefined;`), and the `readonly degradation?: RenderDegradation | undefined;` pattern resolves to that single declaration — i.e. the variant ends at `degradation` with no functional-record field. Combined with r7 (the record reaching DocsContent) and r13 (renderContent reading only markdown/sections/degradation), the host→webview hop the LLD adds is genuinely absent today.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r9","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"StructuredRenderer<T> is exported from vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-panel.ts and its RETURN TYPE is the widened object form '{ el: unknown; degradation?: { degraded: boolean; notice?: string } | undefined }' — not the HLD sc2 sketch's bare HTMLElement — which is the signature the LLD's renderer is written against.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim at vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-panel.ts:155 `export type StructuredRenderer<T> = (record: T) => {`, :157 `readonly el: unknown;` and :159 `readonly degradation?: { readonly degraded: boolean; readonly notice?: string } | undefined;`. The shipped signature is the widened object form, NOT the HLD sc2 sketch's bare HTMLElement, so the LLD is correct to take the signature from LLD-s1, and the `unknown` in its own signatures mirrors the published contract rather than introducing laxity.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r10","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"StructuredRenderer<T> is implemented NOWHERE in code today — no file under vscode-plugin/src contains an annotation of the form ': StructuredRenderer<' — and a test asserts exactly that over docs-review-panel.ts, docs-review-client.ts, docs-sections.ts and markdown-style.ts, which is why the LLD records rewriting that test as a deliberate in-scope contract-test change.","evidence":"The `: StructuredRenderer<` probe returned ZERO code matches — which is the premise, not a probe failure: the type is annotated nowhere under vscode-plugin/src. The pinning test is confirmed by the reads at docs-review-panel.test.ts:432 (it reads docs-review-panel.ts) and :438 `assert.doesNotMatch(src, /:\\s*StructuredRenderer</, ...)` applied over each of the four listed files. So this Story really is the first implementer, and rewriting that assertion is a genuine, scoped contract-test change rather than a relaxation.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r11","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The emitted docs-review shell contains EXACTLY ONE markup-injection site and the test at docs-review-panel.test.ts:698-705 enforces it: it counts matches of /\\.innerHTML\\s*=/ in the generated html and asserts the count is 1, requires that one site to be 'el.innerHTML=marked.parse', and forbids insertAdjacentHTML / outerHTML / document.write. All four assertions fall inside the artifact's cited range 698-705.","evidence":"All FOUR assertions now resolve inside the artifact's cited range 698-705, each read confirmed verbatim: :699 `const injections = html.match(/\\.innerHTML\\s*=/g) ?? [];`, :700 `assert.equal(injections.length, 1, 'exactly one markup-injection site — the markdown body');`, :701 `assert.match(html, /el\\.innerHTML=marked\\.parse/, ...)`, :705 `assert.doesNotMatch(html, /insertAdjacentHTML|outerHTML|document\\.write/, 'no other markup path');`. The single injection site itself is confirmed at docs-review-panel.ts:85. This was the prior review's one MED — the cited range stopped at 703 and under-covered the forbid-list assertion at :705 — and the applied widening to 698-705 resolves it: the citation now covers every assertion the artifact's contract-test section relies on.","action":"none — verified sound; the prior MED is resolved by the applied citation-range widening","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r12","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"stampSlugs pairs rendered headings to posted anchors BY TITLE while advancing a pointer through the index, and leaves an unmatched heading UNSTAMPED — which is the stated reason the LLD requires its placement step to run strictly after stamping (a mutated tree could mis-pair every later heading) and the reason a resolved slug may still have no heading element carrying it.","evidence":"All three probes single-match and all three reads confirmed: :112 `function stampSlugs(root,sections){`, :117 `var j=p;while(j<anchors.length&&anchors[j].title!==txt)j++;` (title pairing via a pointer that only advances) and :118 `if(j<anchors.length){hs[i].id=anchors[j].slug;p=j+1;n++;}}` (stamped only on a match, so an unmatched heading is left unstamped). Both consequences the LLD depends on are confirmed: the strict after-stamping ordering requirement, and the real possibility of a resolved slug with no heading element carrying it — which is why the prepended fallback needs to exist.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r13","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"renderContent's existing call order is renderMarkdownBody → stampSlugs → renderSectionChooser (gated on the stamped count) → renderDegradationNotice → open questions → controls, and the notice already has a two-level precedence (posted degradation, else the body renderer's) — so the LLD's insertion point for placement and its third, lowest-priority notice source are both real positions in a real pipeline.","evidence":"The pipeline is confirmed verbatim: :355 `function renderContent(m){var r=renderMarkdownBody(bodyEl,m.markdown||'');`, :362 `var stamped=stampSlugs(bodyEl,m.sections);`, :363 `renderSectionChooser(secEl,stamped>0?m.sections:{anchors:[]},jumpToSection);`, :366 `renderDegradationNotice(noticeEl,(m.degradation&&m.degradation.degraded)?m.degradation:r.degradation);`. The insertion point the LLD specifies (after :362, before :363) is real, and the existing two-level notice precedence at :366 is exactly the rule the LLD extends with a third, lowest-priority source — so 'a strict extension, not a replacement' is accurate.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r14","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"renderMarkdownBody returns { el, degradation } — degraded:false on the vendored-parse path and degraded:true with the DEGRADE_NOTICE plus a textContent fallback on throw — so the LLD's bodyDegraded argument is taken from an existing returned value rather than re-inferred, and the degraded body genuinely has no heading elements.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim: :76 `export const DOCS_BODY_RENDERER_SOURCE =`, :91 `el.textContent=s;` and :92 `return {el:el,degradation:{degraded:true,notice:DEGRADE_NOTICE}};`. The degraded arm sets textContent on the container and returns the flag, which establishes both LLD premises: `bodyDegraded` comes from an existing return value rather than a re-test, and a degraded body contains no heading elements — so the prepended fallback is the only available placement there, exactly as specified.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r15","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-sections.ts is the SINGLE source of section identity: deriveSectionIndex is ATX-only, skips fenced code with CommonMark fence matching, and falls back to a POSITIONAL 'section-N' slug for a punctuation-only title; createSectionResolver resolves an ofSectionId against the same slugs. The LLD consumes this identity and mints none of its own.","evidence":"All five probes single-match and all four reads confirmed: :47 `const ATX = /^(#{1,6})[ \\t]+(.*)$/;`, :50 `const FENCE = /^[ \\t]{0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)$/;`, :70 `return base.length > 0 ? base : `section-${index + 1}`;` (the deterministic positional fallback), :86 deriveSectionIndex and :136 createSectionResolver. Section identity has exactly one implementation, which is what makes the LLD's 'consume it, mint none' claim meaningful — and what makes the rejected a2's second scanner a real drift risk rather than a stylistic objection.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r16","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The webview-behaviour test harness the LLD extends already exists twice over: loadRenderer builds the body renderer with new Function('marked', DOCS_BODY_RENDERER_SOURCE + '; return renderMarkdownBody') and loadSections does the same for DOCS_SECTIONS_SOURCE against a stub document, with a minimal stubEl of { innerHTML, textContent, className, querySelectorAll }.","evidence":"The harness exists exactly as described, all four probes single-match: test:326 `function stubEl(): Record<string, unknown> {`, :341 `const make = new Function('marked', `${DOCS_BODY_RENDERER_SOURCE}; return renderMarkdownBody;`);`, :576 `function loadSections(...)` and :582 the `new Function('document', ...)` for DOCS_SECTIONS_SOURCE. The LLD's `loadFr()` is therefore a third instance of a shipped, twice-used pattern, and its commitment to prove webview behaviour by execution rather than by string assertion is already this file's stated convention.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r17","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The repo has already shipped the exact malformed-record failure the LLD guards against: childrenOf in the UX companion needed an Array.isArray guard (a `list()` helper) because a malformed `items` threw and took the whole generated document down — the precedent cited for specifying the Array.isArray check on `requirements` up front.","evidence":"The precedent is real and both reads confirm it: src/workflow/artifacts/companion/ux.ts:181 `function childrenOf(el: UxElement): readonly UxElement[] {` and :188 `const list = (v: unknown): readonly UxElement[] => (Array.isArray(v) ? v as readonly UxElement[] : []);`. The repo did have to add an Array.isArray guard for this exact failure mode on this exact kind of body-carried record, which grounds the LLD specifying the `requirements` guard up front rather than leaving it to be discovered.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r18","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"DEF-bfe98ff7f97178cf carries the functional record the LLD's test strategy names as its fixture — doc-level ids of the form 'E20260929bfe98ff7:FR001' and per-item ids of the form 'E20260929bfe98ff7:S002:FR001', grouped per story. The wider ledger counts the LLD cites (4 of 31 DEFs carry the record; HLD 0/30, LLD 0/190, PLAN 0/155; 634 total) were derived by counting .insrc/artifacts/*.json and are NOT re-derivable by a repo grep; they are motivational and no prescribed change depends on their exact value.","evidence":"The fixture is confirmed by the reads: DEF.md:25 `## 2. Functional requirements`, :27 the doc-level `- **E20260929bfe98ff7:FR001** — ...` and :42 the per-item `- **E20260929bfe98ff7:S002:FR001** — ...`. The id grep's matches are all in docs/, which is correct for a cross-artifact premise about a DEF rather than a probe failure. Both id forms and the per-story grouping the renderer must preserve exist in the Epic's own DEF, so the test strategy's instruction to use the real record is actionable. As the premise itself discloses, the ledger counts came from counting .insrc/artifacts/*.json and are not grep-derivable; they are motivational and no prescribed change turns on their exact value.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r19","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The surface's CSS custom properties are scoped to .insrc-term, which is why the shell nests '<body class=\"insrc-term\">' around the surface div, and the body container is a <div id=\"insrc-docs-body\" class=\"insrc-docs-content\"> — so an element the LLD's renderer appends inside that container inherits the --it-* tokens with no new theming scope.","evidence":"Both reads confirmed: docs-review-panel.ts:390 `<body class=\"insrc-term\">` and :401 `<div id=\"insrc-docs-body\" class=\"insrc-docs-content\" aria-label=\"artifact body\"></div>`, both inside the artifact's cited range 389-403. An element appended inside that container therefore inherits the `--it-*` tokens through the existing `.insrc-term` scope, so the LLD's claim that no new theming scope is required holds — the gotcha that cost S001 real time cannot recur here.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"r20","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"openDoc's fail-closed arm posts blocked:true with a placeholder markdown and NO structured record, and the webview renders a 'blocked — not approvable' banner instead of the approve button — the premise behind the LLD's claim that a reviewer who could not read the body is never shown requirements extracted from it.","evidence":"All three reads confirmed: :269 `markdown: `unavailable: ${errText(err)}`,`, :271 `blocked: true,` in that same fail-closed post, and :371 the webview branch rendering 'blocked — not approvable' in place of the approve button. The arm carries no structured record, so the LLD's strongest safety property holds at source: a reviewer who never saw the body is never shown a tidy, authoritative-looking list of commitments extracted from it, and approve stays suppressed.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":20},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-01T13:49:23.980Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
